`normalizeBooks(books)` accepts an array of objects shaped as `{ isbn, title, tags }`. `isbn` must be a unique non-empty string, `title` must be a string, and `tags` must be an array of strings. Return a new array in book order. Each returned book must be a new object whose `tags` is a new array sorted by JavaScript's default string ordering. Preserve duplicate tags. Validate all books before producing the result. Throw `TypeError` for malformed input and `RangeError` for duplicate ISBNs. The input array, book objects, and tag arrays must remain unchanged. An empty input returns an empty array.

Use CommonJS and export exactly `{ normalizeBooks }` with `module.exports`.

Known-bad source:
```javascript
'use strict';

function normalizeBooks(books) {
  if (!Array.isArray(books)) {
    throw new TypeError('books must be an array');
  }

  const seen = new Set();
  for (const book of books) {
    if (
      book === null ||
      typeof book !== 'object' ||
      typeof book.isbn !== 'string' ||
      book.isbn.length === 0 ||
      typeof book.title !== 'string' ||
      !Array.isArray(book.tags) ||
      !book.tags.every((tag) => typeof tag === 'string')
    ) {
      throw new TypeError('invalid book');
    }
    if (seen.has(book.isbn)) {
      throw new RangeError('duplicate isbn');
    }
    seen.add(book.isbn);
  }

  return books.map((book) => ({
    isbn: book.isbn,
    title: book.title,
    tags: book.tags.sort()
  }));
}

module.exports = { normalizeBooks };
```

